      <h2>What we install</h2>
      <p>Three shingle lines, two metal options, one tile option, plus the underlayment, flashing, and ventilation most quotes quietly leave out.</p>

      <h3>Asphalt shingle (80% of what we install)</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>GAF Timberline HDZ</strong>, our default. 30-year architectural shingle, wind-rated to 130 mph, StainGuard Plus algae protection.</li>
        <li><strong>GAF Timberline AS II</strong>, Class-4 impact rated. Costs 12&ndash;18% more up front but often pays back through a 20&ndash;30% insurance discount in Colorado.</li>
        <li><strong>CertainTeed Landmark Pro</strong>, similar performance, different color palette.</li>
        <li><strong>Owens Corning Duration</strong>, if you've had one of their roofs before and want to stay in family.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Metal</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Standing-seam aluminum or steel</strong>, 40&ndash;50 year lifespan. Best for snow shed in higher elevations, also one of the only options that satisfies strict HOA aesthetic boards in Monument.</li>
        <li><strong>Metal shingle</strong>, looks like slate or wood shake from 20 feet away. Higher cost, longer lifespan than asphalt.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Tile</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Concrete or clay tile</strong>, common in some neighborhoods west of I-25 with Mediterranean-style homes. Long-lived but requires careful structural assessment before replacement.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>A realistic timeline</h2>
      <p>For a typical 2,500 sq ft single-story home with asphalt shingle:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Material delivery (early AM). Full tear-off, deck inspection, ice-and-water shield, underlayment.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 2:</strong> Shingle installation, flashing, ridge vent, penetration boots.</li>
        <li><strong>Day 3 (half day):</strong> Cleanup, magnetic sweep of yard and driveway, walk-through with you.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Two-story homes and steeper pitches sometimes add a day. We give you the realistic window when we write the estimate, not the optimistic one.</p>

      <h2>What we don't do on residential</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Roof-over.</strong> Layering new shingles on top of old ones. It's cheaper on paper and always regretted later.</li>
        <li><strong>Partial flashing replacement.</strong> If we're putting down new shingles, the flashing under them is also new.</li>
        <li><strong>Door-to-door sales</strong> after hail. If we're in your neighborhood, it's because someone called us. Not the reverse.</li>
        <li><strong>Storm-chasing pricing.</strong> Our prices don't go up after a hailstorm. The crews and suppliers are the same; the math is the same.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Price range</h2>
      <p>Residential asphalt shingle replacements typically run <strong>$8,500&ndash;$22,000</strong> for a single-family home in the Springs, depending on square footage, pitch, number of penetrations, and shingle grade. Insurance-claim jobs usually cost the homeowner only their deductible.</p>
      <p>We give you a written line-item estimate after the inspection, not a vague "range." You'll see materials cost, labor cost, disposal fees, and permit fees broken out.</p>
